\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.5}{\ignorespaces Capacity-constrained entropic OT between two one-dimensional Gaussian-mixture histograms. Each matrix solves\nobreakspace  {}\textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq-entropic-capacity-constrained-ot}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} with the same marginals and the same squared-distance cost. The left panel is the unconstrained entropic OT solution, corresponding to the density-ratio cap $U_{ij}=+\infty $. The other panels impose $U_{ij}=\kappa a_i b_j$. The red side strip is the source marginal and the blue top strip is the target marginal. As the capacity decreases, more entries saturate and the coupling is forced to spread.}}{230}{figure.10.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:capacity-constrained-ot-1d}{{10.5}{230}{Capacity-constrained entropic OT between two one-dimensional Gaussian-mixture histograms. Each matrix solves~\eqref {eq-entropic-capacity-constrained-ot} with the same marginals and the same squared-distance cost. The left panel is the unconstrained entropic OT solution, corresponding to the density-ratio cap $U_{ij}=+\infty $. The other panels impose $U_{ij}=\kappa a_i b_j$. The red side strip is the source marginal and the blue top strip is the target marginal. As the capacity decreases, more entries saturate and the coupling is forced to spread}{figure.10.5}{}}
