\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.6}{\ignorespaces \emph  {M\'emoli distance profiles expose a computable lower bound for intrinsic GW comparison.} The cat and bunny silhouettes are each discretized by $300$ well-spread farthest-point samples and normalized to unit diameter. A second farthest-point pass selects eight representative cat anchors; their bunny partners are the exact images under the optimal equal-weight assignment with cost $C_{ij}=\Wass  _2^2(\alpha _{x_i},\beta _{y_j})$. Matching colors identify each anchor pair, its transport segment and the two profile histograms in the side columns. The histograms use $20$ bins only for visualization: every entry of $C$ is computed without binning by sorting all $300$ distances in both profiles. The value displayed below is therefore the certified profile lower bound $\mathbb  W_2(\mathfrak  D_\mathbb  {X},\mathfrak  D_\mathbb  {Y})\leqslant \GW  (\mathbb  {X},\mathbb  {Y})$.}}{253}{figure.11.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gromov-memoli-distance-profiles}{{11.6}{253}{\emph {M\'emoli distance profiles expose a computable lower bound for intrinsic GW comparison.} The cat and bunny silhouettes are each discretized by $300$ well-spread farthest-point samples and normalized to unit diameter. A second farthest-point pass selects eight representative cat anchors; their bunny partners are the exact images under the optimal equal-weight assignment with cost $C_{ij}=\Wass _2^2(\alpha _{x_i},\beta _{y_j})$. Matching colors identify each anchor pair, its transport segment and the two profile histograms in the side columns. The histograms use $20$ bins only for visualization: every entry of $C$ is computed without binning by sorting all $300$ distances in both profiles. The value displayed below is therefore the certified profile lower bound $\mathbb W_2(\mathfrak D_\XX ,\mathfrak D_\YY )\leq \GW (\XX ,\YY )$}{figure.11.6}{}}
